              Digital Announces The Industry's Fastest
                   Windows Nt 3d Graphics Adapters
                 With Best-in-class Unix Performance


MAYNARD, Mass., August 6, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the PowerStorm 4D40T, 4D50T, and 4D60T graphics 
accelerators which provide the world's fastest 3D graphics for 
Windows NT and surpasses performance of all comparable UNIX 
offerings. The new accelerators, which are based on industry 
standard interfaces including OpenGL and PCI, operate across 
Digital's Alpha and Pentium Pro workstations under the UNIX and
Windows NT environments. 

     "Digital continues to blaze new trails in the 3D market," said
Philippe Ribeyre, vice president of Digital's Workstation Business 
Segment.  "We were we first to deliver OpenGL, first to adopt PCI 
across our entire workstation and graphics lines, and first to ship 
identical graphics boards on both Windows NT and UNIX. Today we're 
taking the next step by delivering 3D graphics performance and 
functionality for Windows NT that was previously available only on 
extremely high-end workstations, but at half the price." 

     "We are excited about the fast performance of Digital's new
PowerStorm graphics cards," said Shirish Nadkarni, director of 
marketing for Softimage. 

"When combined with Softimage 3D, the PowerStorm series gives 
animators a complete high-performance 3D animation platform that 
allows them to achieve their creative best -- in the shortest time 
possible." 

PowerStorm Graphics Deliver Exceptional Value 

     The new PowerStorm adapters generate high-speed solids and
high-quality anti-aliased lines and dots, as well as tri-linear, 
MIP-mapped texture mapping for incredible image quality used in 
mechanical computer-aided design (MCAD), entertainment, simulated 
and virtual worlds, scientific, and GIS applications.  Texture 
mapping adds an extra dimension of realism to computer graphics by 
allowing scanned or simulated images to be "draped" over solid 
models. A photograph of wood, for example, could be mapped onto a 
sphere, making it look like a wooden ball. 

     PowerStorm graphics accelerators use the Alpha processor's 
powerful floating point capabilities which dramatically accelerates 
geometry calculations. As a result, the PowerStorm adapters provide 
