          J D Edwards Selects Digital as Launch Partner
                     for Windows NT Server

DENVER, August 21, 1996 - Business software developer J.D. Edwards 
today made a strategic announcement with Digital Equipment 
Corporation and Microsoft Corporation where J.D. Edwards' OneWorld 
solutions will run on Digital servers with the Microsoft Windows NT 
operating system.

OneWorld's financial, manufacturing and distribution/logistics 
applications, which are now generally available, are based on J.D. 
Edwards' recently introduced Configurable Network Computing (CNC) 
architecture. Traditionally an IBM AS/400 solution provider, J.D. 
Edwards chose Digital and Microsoft as two of its launch partners in 
the move toward more distributed computing environments.

"We chose Digital as our launch partner for Windows NT because 
together we can offer the most effective OneWorld applications on a 
Windows NT Server," said Mike Iiams, vice president of strategic 
alliances at J.D. Edwards. "We believe Windows NT will be a dominant 
operating system in the years to come and that Digital has the 
world's fastest Windows NT implementation and knows more about 
installing and supporting Windows NT at the enterprise level than 
any other vendor."

"Digital's Alpha/NT platform delivers the high performance, 
scalability and reliability that is required in the information 
technology environment of the future," said Mike Gallup, vice 
president of worldwide systems marketing, Digital Equipment 
Corporation. "Through our strategic alliance with Microsoft, Digital 
is uniquely positioned to assist customers in taking full advantage 
of Windows NT as an enterprise server."

"Digital is excited to be launching OneWorld with J.D. Edwards," 
added Doug Emmons, group manager, business applications software at 
Digital. "The focus of our efforts will be the Windows NT ERP 
Cooperative Technology Center, where OneWorld will be tested and 
characterized for the Windows NT enterprise customer."

"J.D. Edwards' relationship with Microsoft and Digital can bring 
something unique to the client/server marketplace -- rapid, 
economical and predictable implementation of enterprise solutions 
delivered around the world on the Back Office suite of products," 
said Pieter Knook, general manager, business customer marketing at 
Microsoft.

J.D. Edwards' OneWorld is the first software suite to deliver 
platform independent, network- centric technology to the 
multinational, enterprise-wide application market. Called 
Configurable Network Computing, the new architecture utilizes an 
object-based foundation and aims to provide greater flexibility and 
scalability than traditional client/server.

J.D. Edwards & Company is a global leader in enterprise-wide 
business applications. The company's integrated software is designed 
for maximum flexibility and solves business problems for more than 
3,900 customers in 94 countries. With fiscal 1995 revenues of $341 
million and more than 2,700 employees worldwide, privately held J.D. 
Edwards has achieved 53 percent annual compounded revenue growth 
over its 19 years in business. The company provides cross-industry 
solutions, as well as a full complement of industry-specific 
offerings. Additional information about J.D. Edwards can be obtained 
via the Internet at http://www.jdedwards.com.

Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computers to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's Intel and Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.

Microsoft, Windows NT and BackOffice are either registered 
